-- title:
--   Derived series of a subgroup, pair coverage by tuple monoids, and the augmentation kernel of a free group
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a group. For an index set $\iota$, the set $G^\iota$ of tuples $z=(z_i)_{i\in\iota}$ is a group under coordinatewise multiplication. For subgroups $A,B\le G$, $[A,B]$ denotes the subgroup generated by all commutators $xyx^{-1}y^{-1}$ with $x\in A$ and $y\in B$.
--
--   1. **Derived series of a subgroup.** For a subgroup $N\le G$ define recursively
--   $$N^{(0)}=N,\qquad N^{(n+1)}=\bigl[N^{(n)},N^{(n)}\bigr]\qquad(n\in\mathbb{N}).$$
--   If $N$ is normal in $G$, then every $N^{(n)}$ is normal in $G$.
--   2. **Pair coverage.** A submonoid $M\subseteq G^\iota$ *covers $N$ in pairs* if for any two indices $i\neq j$ and any $a,b\in N$ there is $z\in M$ with $z_i=a$ and $z_j=b$.
--   3. **Restriction homomorphism.** For a map $f:\kappa\to\iota$, the monoid homomorphism $G^\iota\to G^\kappa$, $z\mapsto z\circ f$, which re-indexes a tuple along $f$ (a restriction to a sub-family when $f$ is an inclusion).
--   4. **Augmentation of a free group.** For a set $X$ let $F(X)$ be the free group on $X$. The augmentation $\varepsilon_X:F(X)\to\mathbb{Z}$ is the homomorphism to the infinite cyclic group (written multiplicatively) that sends every generator $x\in X$ to the generator $1$; thus $\varepsilon_X(w)$ is the total exponent sum of the word $w$.
--   5. **Augmentation kernel.** $\Delta(X)=\ker\varepsilon_X$, the normal subgroup of $F(X)$ consisting of the words of total exponent sum $0$; it contains, for instance, every quotient $xy^{-1}$ of two generators.
--
--   These notions drive an induction on the number of coordinates: if a submonoid $M\subseteq G^\iota$ over a finite index set contains all constant tuples, admits inverses on every pair of coordinates and covers a normal subgroup $N$ in pairs, then $N^{(n)}$ is contained in the group of elements $g$ for which the tuple with $g$ in coordinate $i$ and $1$ elsewhere is a fraction $uv^{-1}$ with $u,v\in M$, as soon as $|\iota|\le n+2$. The restriction homomorphism passes such a monoid to a sub-family of indices. In the Euclidean Ramsey argument $N$ is the augmentation kernel of the free group whose generators are the points of a Euclidean space.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `Derived N n` is defined by recursion using the commutator subgroup `⁅_,_⁆`, and the instance `derived_normal` gives normality from `[N.Normal]`. `PairCoverage M N` and `restrictionHom f` are stated for `M : Submonoid (ι → G)`. `freeAugmentation X : FreeGroup X →* Multiplicative ℤ` is `FreeGroup.lift (fun _ => Multiplicative.ofAdd 1)`, and `AugKernel X` is its kernel, with an unnamed instance recording that it is normal.
